---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
const cards = [
  ["4504155-war-time-card-file-registration-cards-employees-reco.jpg",
   "Krankenkasse membership and benefits card (Mitglieder- und Leistungskarte Nr. 1), printed in Fraktur. Stamped diagonally DOCUMENT EXPLOITÉ by the French occupation authority."],
  ["4504153-card-file-of-persecutees-in-the-later-french-zone-1.jpg",
   "Municipal questionnaire, Reutlingen, Kreis Reutlingen, Land Württemberg — dated to 1950 with the annotation A.O.K."],
  ["4504154-card-file-of-persecutees-in-the-later-french-zone-2.jpg",
   "Municipal questionnaire, Stadt Pfullingen, Kreis Reutlingen, Württemberg-Hohenzollern — stamped and signed 28 February 1950."],
];
---
<Base title="The Arolsen Cards"
  eyebrow="Reutlingen and Pfullingen, 1941–42 · surveyed 1950"
  dek="Three German index cards, attached to Hedviga's husband in the family tree and never read, place him in a factory in Württemberg at the age of sixteen.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">The <a href={u("/pictures")}>media sweep</a> turned up three record images filed
    under <strong>Ivan Anton Defranceski</strong> (1925–1995) from the
    <strong>Arolsen Archives</strong> — the International Tracing Service, the central archive of
    Nazi persecution. The previous build of this archive listed them as an open question because
    nobody had looked at them. This page is what they say.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>The identification is firm.</strong> All three give the birth as
    <strong>25 June 1925 at Crikvenica</strong> — the same day and place as the
    <a href={u("/certificate")}>1951 Yugoslav birth extract</a>, which records the father of
    Hedviga's children as “Ivan Defranceski, born 25.06.1925, Crikvenica”. One of the three cards
    writes <em>24</em>.6.25; that is a clerical slip against two other cards and a civil
    certificate.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What the cards record</h2>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>Name</th><td><strong>Defranceski</strong>, forename written
        <em>Iwan</em> and <em>Jvan</em> — German renderings of Ivan</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Born</th><td><strong>25 June 1925</strong>, <em>Crikvenica</em> — spelled
        <em>Grikvenica</em> and <em>Cricvenica</em> by two different clerks</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Nationality</th><td><strong><em>Kroatien-Jugoslawien</em></strong> on one card;
        <strong><em>?</em></strong> on another</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Status</th><td><em>led.</em> — <strong>ledig</strong>, single. No wife and no children
        are entered on the insurance card, and every family line is blank.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Lodging</th><td><strong>Pfullingen, Brunnenstraße 7</strong>; the Reutlingen card
        gives the <em>Unterkunftsort</em> simply as Reutlingen</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Employer 1</th><td><strong>Licht u. Kraft</strong>, Reutlingen — apparently the
        electricity and power undertaking. Category <strong><em>Hilfsarb.</em></strong> —
        <em>Hilfsarbeiter</em>, unskilled labourer.
        <strong>21 October 1941 – 16 January 1942.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>Employer 2</th><td>A second Reutlingen employer, same category. The name is written in
        an old German hand and <strong>this archive cannot read it confidently</strong> — it begins
        with what may be <em>Otto</em>. <strong>26 January 1942 – 28 November 194?</strong> — the
        final digit of the year runs into the column rule and is illegible. It is
        <strong>1942 or 1944</strong>, and the difference is ten months against nearly three
        years.</td></tr>
      <tr><th>Insurance</th><td>Registration and deregistration numbers <span class="mono">8143 /
        560</span> for the first post, <span class="mono">905 / 8614</span> for the second</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.1rem">He turned <strong>sixteen</strong> in June 1941 and
    started at Licht und Kraft that <strong>October</strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The most important thing on these cards is what is missing</div>
    <h2>Three blank lines</h2>
    <p class="lede">The 1950 municipal questionnaires exist precisely to establish
    <em>what kind</em> of worker a foreigner had been. Three printed lines do that job. On both
    cards, <strong>all three are empty.</strong></p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th class="warn"><em>Wurde der Arbeiter restlos entlohnt?</em></th>
        <td>“Was the worker paid in full?” — <strong>blank</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th class="warn"><em>Dienstverpflichtet</em></th>
        <td>Compulsorily assigned to service — <strong>blank</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th class="warn"><em>Freier Arbeiter … seit</em></th>
        <td>Free worker, since — <strong>blank</strong></td></tr>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">So the record establishes <strong>that he was there</strong> and
    <strong>what he did</strong>, and is silent on the one question everybody asks first.
    <strong>This archive does not claim he was a forced labourer, and does not claim he went
    voluntarily.</strong> The boxes that would settle it were left empty by a German town clerk in
    1950, five years after the fact.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>Nor does the file title settle it.</strong> The collection is
    catalogued by the Arolsen Archives as <em>“Card file of persecutees in the later French zone and
    of French persecutees in other areas”</em> (DE ITS 2.3.3.1). That is the name of a
    <strong>zone-wide registration file</strong> compiled by the occupation authorities, not a
    finding about any individual in it. Being in the file is not a finding of persecution. The
    second collection is the <em>War Time Card File — registration cards, employees' record books,
    individual correspondence</em> (02020201 oS).</div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The context, stated as context</h2>
    <p>Yugoslavia was invaded on <strong>6 April 1941</strong> and partitioned; the
    <strong>Independent State of Croatia</strong> was declared on <strong>10 April 1941</strong>
    under Axis supervision, and sent workers to the Reich under labour agreements. German recruitment
    of labour from the region <strong>hardened through late 1941 and 1942</strong> as the war in the
    east stalled and the demand for workers became acute.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">Ivan's first registration — <strong>21 October 1941</strong> — sits
    early in that sequence, before the openly coercive round-ups documented from 1942 onward. That
    is a reason to be careful in both directions: it does not make his going voluntary, and it does
    not make it forced. <strong>A sixteen-year-old from an occupied country working in a German
    power undertaking is not a free labour market in any case.</strong></p>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem"><strong>Reutlingen and Pfullingen</strong> are neighbouring towns in
    Württemberg. After 1945 they fell inside <strong>Württemberg-Hohenzollern</strong>, the French
    occupation zone administered from Tübingen — which is why a Croatian teenager's wartime
    employment was surveyed by French-zone municipalities in 1950 and why the insurance card is
    stamped <strong><em>DOCUMENT EXPLOITÉ</em></strong>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The cards</h2>
    <div class="photos">
      {cards.map(([f, cap]) => (
        <figure>
          <img src={u(`/photos/line/${f}`)} alt={cap} loading="lazy" />
          <figcaption>{cap}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Where it sits in the life</h2>
    <p class="lede">This is the earliest documented event in Ivan Defranceski's life after his
    birth, and it reframes the man Hedviga married.</p>
    <table class="kv">
      <tr><th>25 June 1925</th><td>Born at Crikvenica</td></tr>
      <tr><th>21 Oct 1941</th><td><strong>Aged 16</strong> — begins at Licht und Kraft, Reutlingen,
        as an unskilled labourer, lodging at Brunnenstraße 7, Pfullingen</td></tr>
      <tr><th>26 Jan 1942 –<br />28 Nov 194?</th><td>A second Reutlingen employer</td></tr>
      <tr><th>1948</th><td>Back in Croatia: his daughter Ladislava is born at Crikvenica</td></tr>
      <tr><th>17 Dec 1951</th><td>His son Ivan born at Senj hospital; the
        <a href={u("/certificate")}>birth extract</a> gives his trade as
        <strong>electro-mechanic</strong> — which is what a boy who spent the war in a power
        undertaking might well become — and his nationality as <strong>Italian</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>1954</th><td>That extract is drawn and translated for the
        <strong>Supreme Court of South Africa</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><th>30 Oct 1995</th><td>Dies at Senj. <a href={u("/hedviga")}>Hedviga</a> dies at
        Johannesburg on the same date, six years later.</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:1.3rem"><strong>What would settle the rest.</strong> The
    <strong>Arolsen Archives online collections</strong> hold these cards and may hold more under the
    same name — a direct search for <em>Defranceski, born 25.6.1925</em> would show the full file and
    resolve the illegible year. Arolsen answers enquiries from families without charge.
    <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
